    section1Body: "The groom's session is treated as unhurried and detailed — kalgi, brooch or feather placement is checked against the sherwani — while the rest of the wedding party follows a faster, still-coordinated fold.",
    section2Title: "How to book",
    section2Body: "The fastest way to get a quote is a single WhatsApp message with your date, venue pin, guest count and preferred colour direction — we reply with availability and a package recommendation.",
    section3Title: "Who usually books this",
    section3Body: "Enquiries for turban tying at marriage venue in Nainital generally come from people in one of a few situations: actively finalising wedding vendors with a confirmed date, researching options before anything is locked in, or coordinating on behalf of someone else — a family member, or a professional wedding planner managing a client's full vendor list. We treat all of these the same way at the enquiry stage: share what you know so far, even if it's incomplete, and we'll respond with whatever is useful at that stage, whether that's firm availability and pricing or just general guidance while you're still deciding. Planners working across multiple bookings are welcome to reach out directly without the couple needing to be involved in the initial conversation. If you're helping arrange things for someone else, it's worth including their contact details once a specific package and colour direction needs confirming, since that decision is usually easiest to finalise with the person who'll actually be wearing the safa.",
    section4Title: "What's included and how pricing works",
